  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Pb</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> on reaction with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>, produces white precipitate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PbCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Pb</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#10230;</mo><msub><mi>PbCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8595;</mo></math></p><p>This precipitate is soluble in concentrated hydrochloric acid due to formation of tetrachloroplumbate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;ion</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PbCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8595;</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#10230;</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msub><mi>PbCl</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math></p><p>On addition of potassium chromate (K2CrO4 ) solution to lead chloride a yellow precipitate of lead chromate is obtained.&nbsp;</p></blockquote>",
